<section class="formula">
    <h2>Margin vs. Markup: Understanding the Formulas</h2>
    <p>While profit margin and markup both measure profitability from the same dollar gain, they use different denominators and serve distinct accounting purposes.</p>
    
    <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 1.5rem; margin: 1.5rem 0;">
        <div style="background: var(--input-bg); padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">
            <h3 style="margin-top: 0; font-size: 1.1rem; color: #2563eb;">Profit Margin Formula</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem;">Profit margin measures how much profit you keep from every dollar of revenue earned.</p>
            <p><code>Margin % = (Revenue - Cost) / Revenue x 100</code></p>
            <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--text-muted);">Example: If cost is $60 and selling price is $100, profit is $40. $40 / $100 = 40% margin.</p>
        </div>

        <div style="background: var(--input-bg); padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">
            <h3 style="margin-top: 0; font-size: 1.1rem; color: #7c3aed;">Markup Formula</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem;">Markup measures how much you increase the original cost price to set the selling price.</p>
            <p><code>Markup % = (Revenue - Cost) / Cost x 100</code></p>
            <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--text-muted);">Example: If cost is $60 and profit is $40, $40 / $60 = 66.67% markup.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h3>How to Convert Between Margin and Markup</h3>
    <p>To convert a known markup percentage into its equivalent profit margin percentage:</p>
    <p><code>Margin % = Markup % / (1 + Markup %)</code></p>
    <p>To convert a target profit margin percentage into the required markup percentage:</p>
    <p><code>Markup % = Margin % / (1 - Margin %)</code></p>
</section>

